          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Legion Whey+ third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Legion says its products are independently certified by Labdoor, publishes certificates of analysis, and states they are made in NSF-certified facilities. Whey+ is not listed as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport at the time of writing, so drug-tested athletes should choose a product that appears in those databases."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein is in Legion Casein+?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Retailer supplement-facts panels list 21 g of protein per scoop of about 27 to 28 g, with roughly 100 calories, at the time of writing. A separate line lists 25 g of micellar casein, which is the ingredient weight rather than the protein dose, so expect about 21 g per scoop."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What sweetener does Legion use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Legion uses stevia across its proteins. Whey+ lists stevia leaf extract and a stevia extract on dextrin, Casein+ uses Reb M, and Plant+ and the whey concentrate add monk fruit or allulose in some flavors per Legion. None of the protein powders use sucralose or acesulfame potassium, per the labels at the time of writing."
