---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "legion protein powder",
      "legion whey+",
      "legion whey review",
      "legion casein+"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Isolate Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-isolate/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Legion Protein Powder: Clean, Not Cheap</Fragment>
      Legion built its name on natural sweeteners and evidence-based marketing. Here is what Whey+, Casein+ and Plant+ actually deliver per scoop, what Labdoor testing does and does not cover, and which tub is worth your money.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-makes-it">Who Makes Legion</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#whey-label">The Whey+ Label</a>
      <a href="#testing">Labdoor vs NSF</a>
      <a href="#casein">Casein+: Read the Panel</a>
      <a href="#plant-concentrate">Plant+ and Whey Concentrate</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#vs">Legion vs the Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Legion Whey+ is a good grass-fed whey isolate: 22 g of protein in about 100 calories, Irish dairy, stevia instead of sucralose, and a no-return money-back guarantee. It is not a certified sport product, and it costs roughly $2.50 per 25 g of protein, so it suits the recreational lifter who wants a clean label more than the drug-tested athlete or the bargain hunter. The rest of the range is uneven, and Casein+ in particular has less protein per scoop than its marketing suggests.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy <strong>Legion Whey+</strong> if you want a straightforward grass-fed whey isolate with no artificial sweeteners and you are not subject to drug testing. One scoop delivers 22 g of protein in about 100 calories, with roughly 2 g of carbohydrate and almost no fat, per a retailer supplement-facts panel at the time of writing. The whey comes from Irish dairy farms and Legion describes it as Truly Grass Fed certified. It is a credible pick on our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> list for buyers who prioritize a stevia-sweetened label.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is not cheap. At typical retail prices of about $65 to $70 for a 30-serving tub, Whey+ costs roughly $2.50 per 25 g of protein. That is around 70 percent more than a mainstream whey blend, and slightly more than a higher-dose certified isolate such as Transparent Labs. You are paying for the sourcing and the natural sweetener system rather than for extra protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Legion's testing story is also weaker than its marketing language suggests. The brand says its products are independently certified by Labdoor and made in NSF-certified facilities. Neither of those is the same as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport product certification, which is what drug-tested athletes need. If that matters to you, look elsewhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the range: <strong>Plant+</strong> is a solid pea and rice blend with 24 g of protein per serving. The plain <strong>whey concentrate</strong> is Legion's budget option at 20 g. <strong>Casein+</strong> lists 21 g of protein on its supplement-facts panel, fewer than the 25 g headline figure that appears in some places, which is the single most important thing to know before buying it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Legion Whey+ at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">22 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop (label)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Stevia</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sweetener; no sucralose</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~$2.50</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein, approx.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Legion
          </h2>

          <p>
            Legion Athletics was founded by Mike Matthews, a fitness author best known for the book Bigger Leaner Stronger, who used the success of his books to launch the supplement company and remains its chief executive. The brand's identity is evidence-based marketing: product pages lean on study citations and long explanations of why each ingredient is there, and the company has long promoted itself as using only natural sweeteners and flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            That positioning shows up on the labels. Every Legion protein is sweetened with stevia, often alongside monk fruit or allulose, rather than sucralose or acesulfame potassium. Flavors come from natural flavoring, and the protein sources are named specifically: Irish grass-fed whey isolate in Whey+, micellar casein from Irish grass-fed milk in Casein+, PURIS pea and organic brown rice in Plant+.
          </p>

          <p>
            Legion also offers a money-back guarantee that does not require you to return the product, according to its product pages. That lowers the risk of trying a flavor you might dislike, which matters more than usual here because stevia-sweetened powders divide buyers. Some people find stevia's aftertaste noticeable; others prefer it to sucralose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Legion sells primarily through its own website, but its proteins are also stocked by large retailers including Vitacost, iHerb, GNC and Kohl's, and by Amazon. Pricing and availability vary a lot between these channels, and several flavors were listed as temporarily unavailable at major retailers at the time of writing, so it pays to compare before ordering.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Legion Protein Range, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Legion sells four protein powders at the time of writing: Whey+, a cheaper whey concentrate, Casein+ and Plant+. They are ranked below for the job of hitting a daily protein target, followed by two alternatives from other brands. Figures come from Legion's product pages and retailer supplement-facts panels at the time of writing and vary by flavor, so check the tub you buy. The ranking is built on label analysis, testing status and approximate cost per 25 g of protein, not on our own lab or taste tests.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whey-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Whey+ Label, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            A French Vanilla supplement-facts panel at a large retailer lists a 27.2 g scoop, 22 g of protein, 100 calories, 2 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar with no added sugar, and zero fat, with about 30 servings in a 1.8 lb tub, at the time of writing. That is roughly 81 percent protein by weight, which is normal for a flavored isolate. Other flavors run slightly heavier per scoop; Legion and some retailers describe the protein as 22 to 23 g depending on flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list is short: whey protein isolate, natural flavors, xanthan gum, sunflower lecithin, salt and stevia. The same panel also lists a small amount of maltodextrin, about 1 g, and a stevia extract carried on dextrin. Neither matters nutritionally, but buyers who expect a pure isolate with nothing added should know they are there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Twenty-two grams of whey isolate supplies roughly 2.2 to 2.4 g of leucine, since whey runs about 10 to 11 percent leucine. That clears the threshold usually cited for a full muscle-building response in a single serving for most adults. In other words, one scoop of Whey+ does the job; you do not need a scoop and a half as you might with a 20 g isolate.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Label item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Legion Whey+</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical mainstream whey</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-25 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Irish grass-fed whey isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey concentrate and isolate blend</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Testing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Labdoor-certified brand (per Legion)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Buyer feedback on Whey+ is mostly positive about flavor, with the dessert-style options such as Cinnamon Cereal and Chocolate Peanut Butter drawing the most praise. The recurring complaint is mixing: some reviewers describe clumping in a shaker bottle, which a blender or a shaker with a whisk ball usually fixes. That is buyer feedback, not something we tested.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Labdoor Testing vs NSF Certification
          </h2>

          <p>
            Legion's pages describe the brand as independently certified by Labdoor, a company that buys supplements and tests them for label accuracy and contaminants, and say its products are made in NSF-certified, FDA-inspected facilities. Both statements are worth something. Neither is the same as a product sport certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            A facility that is NSF certified for good manufacturing practice has passed audits of its processes. That is a baseline for any reputable supplement factory. It does not mean the specific Legion product has been tested for substances banned in sport. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport are separate programs that test finished product lots for banned substances and are what anti-doping guidance tells athletes to look for.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Certified facility is not a certified product.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you compete in a drug-tested sport, search the exact product in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases. If it is not listed, it is not certified for your purposes, no matter what the facility holds.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For a recreational lifter, Labdoor testing plus certificates of analysis covers the most important question, which is whether the protein on the label is in the tub. Legion publishes certificates of analysis on its product pages, which is better than many brands. It simply does not give you the banned-substance assurance that Momentous or Thorne provide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some retailer listings for Legion proteins also carry a California Proposition 65 warning about lead. That warning is a disclosure triggered at a very low threshold and appears on many cocoa-flavored and plant-based powders; it is not proof of an unsafe dose. If heavy metals concern you, the brand's certificates of analysis are the place to look, and our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains what the numbers mean.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="casein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Casein+: Read the Supplement Panel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Casein+ is micellar casein from Irish grass-fed milk, sweetened with Reb M, a purified stevia compound, and including a small amount of MCT powder. Micellar casein digests slowly, which is why people take it before bed. On paper, it is a well-built product with a clean label.
          </p>

          <p>
            The number to watch is protein per scoop. A Dutch Chocolate supplement-facts panel at a major retailer lists a 28 g scoop with 100 calories and <strong>21 g of protein</strong>, alongside a line reading 25 g of micellar casein protein, at the time of writing. The 25 g figure is the weight of the casein ingredient; the 21 g figure is the protein it actually provides. Some reviews and parts of Legion's own page quote 25 or 26 g, which overstates the dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            At around $70 for roughly 25 servings, that works out to about $3.30 per 25 g of protein, which is expensive for casein. Mainstream micellar caseins with 24 g of protein per scoop typically cost much less, and Legion's own comparison table on the Casein+ page lists competitors at about $1.30 to $1.45 per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want casein specifically from grass-fed Irish milk with no artificial sweetener, Casein+ is one of very few options, and it is a fine product. If you just want a slow protein before bed, compare it with the picks in our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide, where larger doses cost less per gram. Research on bedtime casein often uses 30 to 40 g, so with Casein+ you would need close to two scoops.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plant-concentrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Plant+ and the Whey Concentrate
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plant+</h3>

          <p>
            Plant+ combines PURIS pea protein with organic brown rice protein, which Legion lists as 17.6 g and 6.4 g respectively, for 24 g of protein per serving in about 130 to 140 calories depending on flavor. Legion says the peas are grown in North America and certified glyphosate-free. Sweetening comes from stevia, with monk fruit and allulose in some flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is a strong plant dose: 24 g of pea and rice protein supplies enough leucine for most adults in one serving, which many 20 g plant powders do not. At about $50 for 20 servings direct, Plant+ costs roughly $2.60 per 25 g of protein, mid-premium for a vegan powder. Like Whey+, it is Labdoor-tested per Legion but not NSF Certified for Sport. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide has certified and cheaper alternatives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whey Protein (Concentrate)</h3>

          <p>
            Legion's newer, cheaper whey uses Avonlac 282 whey protein concentrate from cows never treated with rBST, according to the brand, and delivers 20 g of protein in about 120 calories per serving. Sweetening follows the house formula of stevia-family sweeteners and natural flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concentrate carries a little more lactose, fat and carbohydrate than isolate, which matters only if dairy sugar upsets your stomach. At around $45 per tub at retail, it lands at roughly $2 per 25 g of protein, though serving counts vary by flavor. That makes it Legion's value option, but it is still more expensive than mainstream whey blends with bigger scoops. Choose it if you want the Legion label and sweetener system for less money; otherwise a big-brand whey costs less.
          </p>
        </div>

