                  A French Vanilla supplement-facts panel lists 22 g of protein per 27.2 g scoop, with about 100 calories, 2 g of carbohydrate and no fat, at the time of writing. Legion and some retailers describe 22 to 23 g depending on flavor, so check the panel on the tub you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Legion Whey+ third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Legion says its products are independently certified by Labdoor, publishes certificates of analysis, and states they are made in NSF-certified facilities. Whey+ is not listed as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport at the time of writing, so drug-tested athletes should choose a product that appears in those databases.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Legion Casein+?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Retailer supplement-facts panels list 21 g of protein per scoop of about 27 to 28 g, with roughly 100 calories, at the time of writing. A separate line lists 25 g of micellar casein, which is the ingredient weight rather than the protein dose, so expect about 21 g per scoop.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener does Legion use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Legion uses stevia across its proteins. Whey+ lists stevia leaf extract and a stevia extract on dextrin, Casein+ uses Reb M, and Plant+ and the whey concentrate add monk fruit or allulose in some flavors per Legion. None of the protein powders use sucralose or acesulfame potassium, per the labels at the time of writing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Legion protein sold on Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Legion's proteins are sold on Amazon as well as on Legion's own site and at retailers such as Vitacost, iHerb, GNC and Kohl's. Prices and stock vary by channel and flavor, and Legion's own site often has bundle discounts, so compare before buying a multi-tub order.
                </p>
